mally a consideration  for political candidates that's not the  general status quo for  our people in our ways of behaving rcmp  say break  is in custody he'll be in court for a  bail hearing thursday  ross lord global news  in france the court of appeal has ruled  a canadian wanted in connection with a  1980 bombing outside a paris synagogue  must stand trial  three years ago lower court judges freed  hassan diab over a lack of evidence  the 67 year old's lawyer says he will  challenge  today's ruling it flies in the face  of the existing evidence you'll recall  that this is a case in canada that  was based on a sheer opinion a  handwriting opinion  that the world's leading experts all  unanimously agreed was completely  unreliable  our cmp officers arrested diab in 2008  at the request of france  following a lengthy court battle he was  extradited and spent three years in  prison there  in 2018 french judges dismissed the case  and ordered diab's release he returned  home to ottawa  dieb has denied involvement in the  attack that killed four people and  injured  more than 40 others cloris leachman an  actor who is fearless and funny  has passed away do you know how to  remove chocolate stains  yes i do oh good  leachman started her career on stage and  broke into film and television in 1947  and she kept working for decades you may  know her best from tv roles in  the mary tyler moore show and the facts  of life or  on the big screen in films like young  frankenstein  and the last picture show for which she  won an oscar  no other actress has been nominated for  more emmys and she tied for the most  wins  leachman passed away from natural causes  last night at her home in california  she was 94.  pandemic probe still ahead a new covet  test happening behind closed doors  in china  indonesia's most active volcano mount  merapi is erupting again  at least 150 people had to leave their  homes and there have been  no reports of injuries or deaths the  volcano has erupted repeatedly in recent  weeks  the area has been under the country's  second highest alert level since  november hong kong is using a drastic  new tactic to control covet 19  the government has begun ambush style  lockdowns closing off entire  neighborhoods with no warning  everyone within the area gets tested for  covet 19. hong kong has been struggling  to contain a fourth wave of the virus  nearly 40 percent of all infections  there have been recorded  in the last two months and china is  using a new kind of covid test one that  sounds a little uncomfortable  scientists say data has shown traces of  the coronavirus last  longer in the digestive tract than in  the nose or throat so  china is using anal swabs to test for  covid19 yes anal  swabs only certain high risk groups are  being tested this way as you can imagine  the invasive technique is not  sitting well with everyone china has  taken other extreme measures to stop the  spread of covid19 locking down  entire cities and provinces to contain  outbreaks  tighter restrictions are being reimposed  now ahead of lunar new year  celebrations are the kids  all right next the pandemic's impact on  teen mental health  the work never stops on the  international space station astronauts  michael hopkins and victor glover who  arrived there in november  ventured outside to finish installing a  european space agency platform  it will allow astronauts to conduct  science and technology demonstrations  outside the space station  they worked for almost seven hours and  at one point joked they wished they  could pop into the airlock for some  snacks  and bacon well floating in space would  be a welcome  escape from what we're all dealing with  here on earth the pandemic has now  lasted long enough for research to be  done  on what only illnesses deaths and  restrictions are doing to our mental  health  tonight heather urix west looks at the  impact on teenagers  normally kyra cato is in the gym  training in gymnastics more than 30  hours a week  but since november her gym has been  closed it has been pretty frustrating  but um i try to keep a positive  outtake outlook on everything but it  gets hard  i opened up the other night  asking questions about how they were  feeling and i had a lot of  responses that were actually like quite  heart-wrenching  coach janna ball says many of her  athletes are struggling right now with  feelings of anxiety and  depression unsure of how long they'll  have to wait  before things start to feel normal again  we're  seeing changes over the months that  we've done our study we're seeing  and most of it is going in the wrong  direction  a calgary research project launched at  the beginning of the school year  surveyed more than 2  thousand high school students in calgary  and edmonton  in september it found 17 percent or 1 in  6 were experiencing critical levels of  poor mental health by december when  students were surveyed again  that number had risen to more than 20  percent or about  one in five across the country mental  health professionals are seeing demand  for their services rise  calls to the kids help phone more than  doubled in 2020  indicating that not only are more young  people struggling  but more people are reaching out for  help the most important thing is that  our young people are talking about what  they're experiencing and  they're going through the process of  trying to figure out some solutions for  themselves  that can help them get through this  pandemic fifteen-year-old jane sherwood  says she's using  art and music to get through these  difficult times and talking to her  parents more  as well i've become a lot more closer  with my family through this and so  i think that we've kind of like  established an uh like a new kind of  like openness with that kind of thing  which i think has helped a lot learning  how to be resilient  during an incredibly challenging time  heather urex west global news  calgary and that is global national for  this wednesday i'm donna friesen  today is international holocaust  remembrance day it's been 76 years since  the liberation of auschwitz the most  notorious of nazi death camps  more than 1.1 million people the  majority of them jewish died in that  camp  just a fraction of the more than 6  million jews killed in the holocaust  israel marked the day with a virtual  ceremony  in the uk the london eye was among the  sites bathed in purple light  and in warsaw poland an 88 year old  holocaust survivor laid a wreath  in the former warsaw ghetto germany's  president said today  the greatest danger for all of us begins  with forgetting thanks for watching hope  to see you here again tomorrow  take care of yourselves
 

